## `scheduler.mjs` implementation details

Added idle/accounting helpers:

```js
isIdle()
waitForIdle()
notifyIdleWaiters()
```

Idle means:

```text
queue.length === 0
pending.size === 0
inFlight === 0
```

`waitForIdle()` resolves immediately if scheduler is already idle. Otherwise it stores a waiter until the scheduler becomes idle.

`notifyIdleWaiters()` is called after work-removal paths:

```text
complete()
fail()
cancel()
cancelAll()
cancelBySession()
```

Important architecture boundary:

```text
scheduler.mjs does not own shutdown policy.
scheduler.mjs only exposes idle/accounting state for inference.mjs to use.
```

## `inference.mjs` implementation details

### Prompt admission gate

Added:

```js
assertPromptAdmissionAllowed(sessionId)
```

`prompt()` now checks lifecycle gates both before and after readiness:

```text
assertPromptAdmissionAllowed(sessionId)
await ensureModelReady()
assertPromptAdmissionAllowed(sessionId)
create request
enqueue request
```

Reason:

```text
runtime reset/shutdown or session reset can begin while prompt is awaiting readiness.
```

This closes a pre-admission race without turning pre-readiness waiters into accepted drainable work.

### Shutdown option validation

Added:

```js
validateShutdownOptions(options)
```

Rules:

```text
mode must be "abort", "drain", or "drain-with-timeout".
default mode remains "abort".
timeoutMs is required for "drain-with-timeout".
timeoutMs must be a positive integer.
timeoutMs with "abort" or "drain" rejects.
```

### Active-init shutdown guard

Added:

```js
isInitActive()
```

Current condition:

```text
initInProgress || (initStarted && !initResolved)
```

If shutdown is requested during active initialization, `shutdownRuntime()` rejects with:

```text
Model initialization is in progress
```

Important: this branch does not cancel active init. It only rejects shutdown during active init.

### Shutdown helpers

Added internal helpers:

```js
cancelRequestsForShutdown(reason)
finalizeWorkerShutdown()
shutdownAbort()
shutdownDrain()
waitForSchedulerIdleOrTimeout(timeoutMs)
shutdownDrainWithTimeout(timeoutMs)
```

Design principle:

```text
helper extraction is behavior-driven and minimal.
this branch is not a broad cleanup or modularization pass.
```

## Important behavioral notes

### Intake vs dispatch

This branch preserves a strict distinction:

```text
runtimeShuttingDown blocks new prompt intake.
scheduler readiness controls dispatch of already accepted work.
```

For `drain`, the implementation intentionally does **not** call:

```js
scheduler.setReady(false)
```

at shutdown start.

Reason:

```text
accepted queued work must be allowed to dispatch and finish during drain.
```

For `abort`, `scheduler.setReady(false)` is still correct and preserved.

For `drain-with-timeout`, `scheduler.setReady(false)` is called only after the timeout expires, when the branch deliberately switches to abort-like fallback cancellation for remaining accepted work.

### Accepted work definition

A prompt is accepted drainable work only after it has a request object and is queued/pending/running in scheduler accounting.

A prompt waiting inside `ensureModelReady()` is still pre-admission work and is not drainable accepted work.

### Runtime remains terminal after shutdown

All shutdown modes leave the runtime shut down. This branch does not implement restart after shutdown.

### Reset remains separate

`resetModel()` remains semantically separate from shutdown.

```text
resetModel(): cancels work, disposes/rebuilds worker, reinitializes runtime.
shutdownRuntime(): disposes/terminates worker and remains off.
```

No new `resetModel()` semantics were intentionally introduced.

## Native / worker caveats

### Native prompt preemption is not implemented

`drain-with-timeout` is a parent-side timeout.

It guarantees:

```text
parent stops waiting for accepted scheduler work after timeout.
remaining accepted requests are locally canceled/rejected.
streams are closed/canceled locally.
done promises reject with "Runtime shutdown timeout".
worker shutdown is requested afterward.
```

It does **not** guarantee:

```text
node-llama-cpp generation stops immediately.
shutdownRuntime() returns within timeoutMs.
worker/native calls are interrupted.
```

Current worker cancellation still depends on the existing worker behavior. If `session.prompt(...)` is already inside a native call, the worker may continue until that call returns.

### Future native cancellation branch

A later branch should wire native prompt cancellation where supported by `node-llama-cpp`, likely through:

```text
parent cancel / abort / drain-timeout
-> worker cancel message
-> AbortController.abort(reason)
-> session.prompt(..., { signal })
```

Suggested future branch boundary:

```text
worker-native-cancellation-boundary-v1
```

or:

```text
worker-prompt-abort-signal-v1
```

This belongs with worker operation serialization / native-operation cancellation boundary work, not with `drain-shutdown-v1`.

## Real smoke-test design correction

An earlier real smoke mode incorrectly treated plain `drain` as proof that real prompt generation would complete within a fixed test timeout.

That was corrected.

Final v3 real smoke test now matches the implemented branch:

```text
real-drain-idle:
  verifies real init + drain shutdown + worker/model shutdown with no accepted work.

real-abort-parent-cancel:
  verifies parent-side cancellation/rejection and worker/model shutdown under abort.

real-drain-timeout-parent-cancel:
  verifies parent-side timeout cancellation/rejection and worker/model shutdown under drain-with-timeout.
```

Optional extended/manual mode remains:

```text
real-drain-queued-completes-extended
```

That optional mode tests real prompt completion during plain drain, but it is not part of the branch-scoped real orchestrator because real model speed/generation behavior is outside this branch's core guarantee.

The final smoke test prints:

```text
[SMOKE] version: drain-shutdown-v1-branch-scoped-real-modes-v3
[SMOKE] file: <actual file path>
[SMOKE] orchestrator modes: ...
[SMOKE] real orchestrator modes: ...
```

to prevent stale-file confusion.

